cd_ws="""
Controls the burning speed for CDs.

Most modern drives and quality blank media
tend to burn best around 16x, offering a good
balance between speed and overall reliability.

Lower speeds can sometimes improve pit geometry
and readability on older CD players, retro
consoles, car stereos, and other messy ass hardware.
"""
dvd_ws="""
Controls the burning speed for DVDs.

DVDs have higher capacity than CDs, so burn speed
can vary more depending on media quality and drive performance.

8x is probably your best bet, while 16x can work well on
higher quality discs and modern drives. Both offer a good
balance between speed and reliability.
"""
cd_rs="""
Controls the read / ripping speed for CDs

For clean, undamaged discs, MAX speed is USUALLY fine.

If a disc is producing read errors, lowering the speed
can help your reader / writer recover data more reliably and
reduce skipped sectors.

Keep in mind these read / ripping modes won't be enough for
heavily damaged discs or discs that are prone to read errors often;
using specialized software like ddrescue are recommended.
"""
dvd_rs="""
Controls the read / ripping speed for DVDs.

Same idea as with the CD read speeds, MAX is usually fine for undamaged
discs.

On discs that produce read errors, lowering the speed can help
your reader / writer rip or recover data more reliably and reduce
skipped sectors.
"""
def_drv="""
The optical drive assumed by PABS on startup.

PABS will as usual try to automatically detect the first available
reader / writer at your disposal. If there are no drives / your selected
drive cannot be reached; the app will throw a warning.
"""
write_mode="""
Disc At Once (DAO) writes the entire disc in a
single pass with no gaps, best for audio CDs
and discs that must be read on standalone players.

Track At Once (TAO) writes one track at a time
and allows multi session discs.
"""
verify="""
Re-reads the disc after burning and compares it against
the original data.

Helps catch bad burns and hidden read errors before they
catch you...

This option will ROUGHLY take as long as the original burn did
since PABS is forced to re-read every sector of the disc.
"""
eject="""
Automatically ejects the disc tray after a
successful burn or rip.

Also avoids the Windows Explorer slowdown
that can happen when a disc is left inserted
during shutdown / startup.
"""
mode_select="""
Sends a SCSI MODE SELECT command to configure
drive parameters before burning.

Only needed for certain older drives. Leave
this off unless you know your drive really
really needs it.
"""
iso_mode="""
Selects the filesystem format for discs.

ISO9660 is the oldest and most compatible format, supported by nearly every OS
and reader / writer. Depending on the compatibility level used, it may restrict
filename length and character support.

Joliet (9660) is Microslop's format that extends ISO9660 with longer filenames and
UNICODE support, mainly for improved Windows compatibility. Most modern OSes support
Joliet (9660) without issues or any concerns.

UDF (Universal Disk Format) is the modern standard for DVDs and Blu-ray discs.
Although PABS unironically can't burn Blu-ray's, UDF still supports UNICODE, long
file names and generally sizes over 4GBs without flaw. However it is less likely to
be supported on older / legacy devices or hardware.

Hybrid; which adds all of the previously mentioned ISO modes into one and depending
on the OS or system reading the disc / file. It will pick the best / most suitable
option to work with, example how Windows might pick Joliet (9660) for regular DVDs
or CDs and how some legacy systems (example older NT based OSes) will prefer 9660
for usage.
"""
ffmpeg_wav="""
How PABS saves converted non-wav files with FFmpeg

When enabled (assuming you have FFmpeg in PATH
that PABS can use), the app will cache the converted
files to the __cache directory and wipe them when done.

When this setting is disabled; PABS will cache all of the converted
files in your systems RAM and release it when you remove the selected
tracks or close the app itself.
"""
ffmpeg_path="""
How PABS uses FFmpeg.

PABS will (if can) use FFmpeg to convert any compatible codec with FFmpeg
into raw PCM data, since audio discs / CDs mostly require a specific WAV codec
to function or be burned in general. PABS will automatically detect any presence
of FFmpeg in your systems PATH for usage.

If however you do not have FFmpeg in PATH or want a different ffmpeg.exe to be used;
you can manually browse and select your desired option and save it.
"""
vol_label="""
Default volume label written into the disc filesystem.

Maximum 11 characters for ISO9660 on Windows (CD / DVD).
The only times you'd see this name is when in Windows
Explorer or something.
"""